{0 Recipes}

Short setups for tasks that most apps have: Tailwind, SVG, JSON codecs for the
two compilers, and a server. For navigation, see {!page-router}; for a new
platform, see {!page-platform}.

{1:tailwind Tailwind}

Silica has no CSS layer of its own. An app uses Tailwind through a dune rule.
Mini Linear has the rule in [linear/client/dune]. The Tailwind command line
([@tailwindcss/cli], from [node_modules]) reads [input.css], finds the class
names in the app source, and writes [app.css]:

From [linear/client/dune]:
{@dune skip[
(rule
 (targets app.css)
 (deps
  input.css
  themes.css
  package.json
  ../../ui/silica-ui.css
  (glob_files ../../ui/*.ml)
  (glob_files ../app/shared/*.ml)
  (glob_files ../app/shared/*.mlx)
  (source_tree node_modules))
 (action
  (setenv
   RAYON_NUM_THREADS
   1
   (run
    node
    node_modules/@tailwindcss/cli/dist/index.mjs
    -i
    input.css
    -o
    app.css))))
]}

The input file turns off the automatic scan and names the source folder. In the
dune build folder, the automatic scan also reads earlier outputs, so the CSS
would depend on what was built before.

{@css file=../linear/client/input.css[
@import "tailwindcss" source(none);
@import "../../ui/silica-ui.css";
@import "./themes.css";
@source "../app/shared";

@theme inline {
  --color-warning: var(--warning);
  --color-warning-surface: var(--warning-surface);
}
]}

esbuild then bundles [app.css] with the client code. It adds a hash to each
file name, and the metafile gives the page the URL of each file (see
{{!section-hosting}Hosting}).

Write each class as a complete string literal. Tailwind reads the source text,
so a name made at run time, such as ["bg-" ^ colour], gets no CSS. For a class
that depends on a condition, use {!Silica.Class.join}. It drops empty strings:

{@ocaml[
# Silica.Class.join [ "grow truncate"; (if true then "line-through" else "") ];;
- : string = "grow truncate line-through"
# Silica.Class.join [ "grow truncate"; (if false then "line-through" else "") ];;
- : string = "grow truncate"
]}

Colours, radius, shadow, font and the spacing unit come from themes: the
[themes.css] file that the [themes] rule writes, and the [@theme inline] block
for the app tokens of mini Linear. See {!page-theming}.

The components of [silica-ui] use theme tokens. To get their CSS, import
[silica-ui.css] in the Tailwind input, as the second line of the input above
does. That file has an [@source] line for the components, so the rule also
depends on [ui/*.ml].

{1:svg SVG}

Write SVG in mlx as any other element. Use the camelCase name of an attribute
when SVG has one, such as [viewBox]. Any other name with an upper-case letter
prints with dashes: [strokeWidth] prints as [stroke-width]. An icon is a
function that gives a node:

{@mlx file=examples/shared/tutorial_icon.mlx[
let check () =
  <svg viewBox="0 0 16 16" width="16" height="16">
    <path d="M3 8l3 3 7-7" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" strokeWidth="2" />
  </svg>
]}

{@ocaml[
# Silica.Render.to_string (Tutorial_icon.check ());;
- : string =
"<svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" width=\"16\" height=\"16\"><path d=\"M3 8l3 3 7-7\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\"></path></svg>"
]}

In the browser, the [svg] element and its children get the SVG namespace. The
children of [foreignObject] are HTML again. Names that start with [xlink:] or
[xml:] get their namespace too. There is no node for a raw SVG or HTML string.

{1:text Text that is not ASCII}

OCaml strings are bytes. Native code and the server read a literal such as
["Café"] as UTF-8, but Melange turns each byte of a plain literal into one
JavaScript character, so the browser shows ["CafÃ©"]. Write such text in
shared code as a [{js|...|js}] literal. Melange reads it as Unicode text, and
native OCaml reads it as an ordinary quoted string, so both sides get the same
text and hydration matches:

{@ocaml[
# {js|Café|js} = "Caf\195\169";;
- : bool = true
]}

Text that comes from JSON (Remote answers, Live states) and from inputs is
already correct on both sides. The site test [text that is not ASCII is a
{js|...|js} literal] fails on a plain literal with such text in the shared code
of the site, silica-ui, mini Linear and the examples.

{1:json JSON: two stanzas}

The server and the browser use different JSON libraries: [jsonkit] for native
code and [jsonkit-melange] for Melange. App code that derives JSON functions
with [[@@deriving json]] must compile with the matching library. So the shared
code of an app is compiled by two dune stanzas.

The shared folder holds the code and no stanza:

{@dune file=../example/app/shared/dune[
(dirs)
]}

The native stanza copies the shared files and uses [silica.jsonkit]:

{@dune file=../example/app/native/dune[
(copy_files ../shared/*.{ml,mlx})

(library
 (name silica_example_app_native)
 (wrapped false)
 (modes native)
 (libraries silica silica.jsonkit jsonkit)
 (preprocess
  (pps jsonkit.ppx silica.ppx)))
]}

The Melange stanza copies the same files and uses [silica-jsonkit-melange]:

{@dune file=../example/app/melange/dune[
(copy_files ../shared/*.{ml,mlx})

(library
 (name silica_example_app_melange)
 (wrapped false)
 (modes melange)
 (libraries silica silica-jsonkit-melange jsonkit-melange)
 (preprocess
  (pps jsonkit-melange.ppx silica.ppx melange.ppx)))
]}

Both libraries have the module [Silica_jsonkit], so the shared code is the same
for the two stanzas. [Silica_jsonkit.codec] makes a {!Silica.Codec.t} from the
derived functions:

{@ocaml file=../example/app/shared/example_model.ml[
open Jsonkit.Primitives

module Item = struct
  type t = { id : int; title : string; price_cents : int; tags : string list }
  [@@deriving json]
end

module Counter_event = struct
  type t = Incr | Decr | Reset [@@deriving json]
end

let item_endpoint =
  Silica.Remote.declare ~path:"/api/item" ~input:Silica.Codec.int
    ~output:(Silica_jsonkit.codec ~to_json:Item.to_json ~of_json:Item.of_json)

let counter_state = Silica.Codec.int

let counter_event =
  Silica_jsonkit.codec ~to_json:Counter_event.to_json
    ~of_json:Counter_event.of_json

let votes_topic = Silica.Topic.declare ~name:"votes" ~state:Silica.Codec.int

let vote_endpoint =
  Silica.Remote.declare ~path:"/api/vote" ~input:Silica.Codec.unit
    ~output:Silica.Codec.int
]}

For simple types you do not need [jsonkit]: {!Silica.Codec} has codecs for
strings, numbers, lists, options and pairs.

{1:hosting Hosting with the server package}

The package [silica.server] has the server logic: routes for pages, Remote
calls and Live sockets, sessions, CSRF tokens, origin checks, hashed assets and
security headers. It does not open a port. An adapter connects it to an HTTP
library. [Silica_server.Make (Io)] takes the concurrency of that library.

The repository has one adapter, over [tiny_httpd], in [example/server_adapter/].
This server serves the greeting component of the {{!page-tutorial}tutorial}:

{@ocaml file=examples/server/tutorial_server.ml[
module Adapter = Silica_example_adapter
module Server = Adapter.Server

let read_file path = In_channel.with_open_bin path In_channel.input_all

let greeting _context name = Ok ("Hello, " ^ name)

let () =
  let handlers = [ Server.implement Tutorial_api.greeting greeting ] in
  let assets =
    match
      Silica_server.Assets.of_metafile ~prefix:"/static/"
        (read_file "_build/default/client/meta.json")
    with
    | Ok assets ->
        assets
    | Error message ->
        failwith message
  in
  let config =
    Silica_server.default_config ~origins:Silica_server.Localhost
      ~secret:(Silica_server_kit.Random_source.hex_id ~bytes:32)
  in
  let server =
    Server.create config ~assets
      ~read:(Adapter.read_dir "_build/default/client/dist")
      ~handlers ~islands:[] ~topics:[]
      ~page:(fun (page : Server.page) ->
        Server.render_page_settled
          (fun _url -> Tutorial_greeting.component)
          ~status:(fun _ -> 200)
          ~scripts:[ page.asset "tutorial_client.js" ]
          ~csrf:page.csrf ~nonce:page.nonce ~handlers ~context:page.context
          page.url
      )
      ()
  in
  Adapter.run ~port:8080 server
]}

The parts:

- [Server.implement] connects an endpoint to a function. The function gets the
  context (the request and the session) and the input.
- [Silica_server.Assets.of_metafile] reads the esbuild metafile. The page asks
  for a file by its name before the hash, such as [tutorial_client.js], and
  gets the URL with the hash.
- [default_config] needs the allowed origins and a secret of at least 16
  bytes. [Localhost] is for development only. In production give the list of
  origins of the site.
- [render_page_settled] runs the Remote calls of the first render on the
  server, and writes the answers into the page. The browser then starts from
  the same state.
- The paths in this file assume a [client/] folder at the root of the project,
  with an esbuild rule as in [example/client/dune].

The dune stanza links the shared code, the server package and the adapter:

{@dune file=examples/server/dune[
(executable
 (name tutorial_server)
 (libraries
  silica_docs_examples_native
  silica.server
  silica.server-kit
  silica_example_adapter))
]}

Sessions live in memory: a server restart ends them. The browser then reloads
the page one time and gets a new session. {!page-server} explains the policy
of the server: sessions, CSRF tokens, origins, assets and Live sockets.
